    {
      "duration_ms": 9500,
      "caption": "A concrete function: one entry block, a GEP, a load and a terminator.",
      "narration": "Here is a real function. One entry block computes an address with getelementptr, loads through it, and ends with a ret terminator.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "h",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "A tiny function",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 5,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "ir",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "llvm",
          "text": "define i32 @second(ptr %a) {\nentry:\n  %p = getelementptr i32, ptr %a, i64 1\n  %v = load i32, ptr %p\n  ret i32 %v\n}",
          "x": 6,
          "y": 18,
          "w": 88,
          "enter": "slide-up",
          "at": 1300
        },
        {
          "id": "hl",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "ir",
          "at": 2100
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 12000,
      "caption": "Every block ends in exactly one terminator, and every value is typed.",
      "narration": "A block ends with exactly one terminator, like ret, br or switch. Phi nodes may only appear at the start of a block, and every value is typed.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "h",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Structural rules",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 5,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "l",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Block = instructions + 1 terminator",
            "Terminators: ret, br, switch, unreachable",
            "phi only at the start of a block",
            "Every value has a type: i32, ptr, <4 x float>",
            "%names are SSA values: defined once"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 18,
          "w": 84,
          "enter": "slide-up",
          "color": "blue",
          "at": 1300
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 9500,
      "caption": "getelementptr only computes an address: base plus scaled indices. It never touches memory.",
      "narration": "The getelementptr instruction is pure address arithmetic: a base pointer plus indices scaled by type sizes. It reads and writes no memory.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "h",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "getelementptr",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 5,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "g1",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "llvm",
          "text": "%p = getelementptr i32, ptr %a, i64 1",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 18,
          "w": 84,
          "enter": "slide-up",
          "at": 1300
        },
        {
          "id": "e1",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "= %a + 1 * sizeof(i32) = %a + 4",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 34,
          "w": 84,
          "enter": "fade",
          "color": "green",
          "at": 2100
        },
        {
          "id": "g2",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "llvm",
          "text": "%q = getelementptr %struct.S, ptr %s,\n          i32 0, i32 2",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 48,
          "w": 84,
          "enter": "slide-up",
          "at": 2900
        },
        {
          "id": "e2",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "index 0 steps over S-sized elements; index 2 picks field 2",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 64,
          "w": 84,
          "enter": "fade",
          "color": "orange",
          "at": 3700
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 8000,
      "caption": "Only load and store access memory; GEP just feeds them an address.",
      "narration": "GEP computes where; load or store does the access. That keeps address math optimizable like any other arithmetic.",
